(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-raas-inhibitors-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Renin-Angiotensin Inhibitors & First-Line Choice Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug added to losartan most increases the risk of hyperkalemia?","choices":["A potassium-sparing diuretic","A dihydropyridine","A beta-1 selective blocker","An alpha-1 blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia with angiotensin receptor blockers is linked to renal disease and potassium-sparing diuretics, both of which keep potassium in. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which statement describes ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors in left ventricular dysfunction?","choices":["Avoid, as they worsen remodeling","Use only after beta blockers fail","Give to all unless contraindicated","Use only when blood pressure is high"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They reduce remodeling, afterload and preload, delay progression of heart failure, and reduce myocardial infarction and hospitalization. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 58-year-old without diabetes has blood pressure 8/4 mmHg above goal and a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio below 300 mg/g. Lifestyle counseling is under way. Which is an appropriate first drug?","choices":["A beta blocker or an alpha-1 blocker","Hydralazine or minoxidil","An ACE inhibitor or a dihydropyridine","Clonidine or guanfacine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Below the combination threshold and without albuminuria, first line is an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker, or a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A patient asks whether the dry cough from her ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor will stop if the dose is lowered. What is accurate?","choices":["It resolves with a lower dose","It stops on switching to captopril","It is not related to dose or agent","It occurs only in the first day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cough is not dose- or agent-related, is more frequent in women, and may require removing the ACE inhibitor if it is bothersome. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why does raising an angiotensin receptor blocker's dose change blood pressure only a little?","choices":["Higher doses induce its metabolism","Receptors saturate at starting doses","It is converted to an inactive form","Higher doses block the receptor less"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. AT1 (angiotensin II type 1) receptors are saturated at starting doses for most agents, so the dose response is nonlinear; adding a diuretic increases the effect more than a dose increase. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient in her third trimester cannot take an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor. Is an angiotensin receptor blocker an acceptable substitute?","choices":["No, it is also avoided then","Yes, it spares the fetus","Yes, at a reduced dose","Only if potassium is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers share the fetal morbidity and mortality and are not given in the second and third trimesters. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient taking lisinopril and amlodipine remains above goal after titration, and adherence and measurement have been confirmed. What is the next step?","choices":["Add a thiazide-like diuretic","Add losartan","Add hydralazine","Swap amlodipine for verapamil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker plus a dihydropyridine, the next step is a thiazide-like diuretic; if pressure is still uncontrolled, it is apparent resistant hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Four days after starting lisinopril, a patient develops rapid swelling of the lips, tongue and throat. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Hyperkalemia from low aldosterone","Bradykinin-mediated angioedema","First-dose hypotension","Sodium and water retention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor angioedema is rare, usually appears in the first week, and is reversible when the drug is removed. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient with hypertension mildly above goal has a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 450 mg/g. Which initial therapy is recommended?","choices":["A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A beta blocker or an alpha-1 blocker","An ACE inhibitor or receptor blocker","Hydralazine or minoxidil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With an albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 300 mg/g or more, the algorithm starts an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or an angiotensin receptor blocker, consistent with their kidney-protective effect. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Besides lowering angiotensin II, what does blocking angiotensin-converting enzyme do to bradykinin?","choices":["Speeds its breakdown","Slows its breakdown","Blocks its receptor","Leaves it unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same enzyme that makes angiotensin II also inactivates bradykinin, so bradykinin accumulates -- the root of both the dry cough and angioedema. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has an intravenous active form?","choices":["Lisinopril","Benazepril","Quinapril","Enalapril"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Enalapril is converted to enalaprilat, which is available as the intravenous form. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why can angiotensin II still be produced in a patient taking an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor?","choices":["Renin makes it directly","The adrenal gland secretes it","Bradykinin converts into it","Other enzymes such as chymase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Trypsin, cathepsin and chymase provide alternative routes to angiotensin II -- the limiting factor of ACE inhibitors that angiotensin receptor blockers get around. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient on lisinopril begins taking ibuprofen daily for knee pain. How do NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) affect the ACE inhibitor?","choices":["They increase its effect","They have no interaction","They cause its cough","They reduce its effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. NSAIDs block the prostaglandin-dependent, bradykinin-mediated relaxation that contributes to the ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's effect, so blood pressure control weakens. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"First-dose hypotension with an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is most common in which patient?","choices":["One with mild, untreated hypertension","One who is sodium depleted","One with type 2 diabetes alone","One younger than 40 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is most common in sodium-depleted patients, in heart failure and in those on several antihypertensive drugs, and occurs with the first dose or an upward titration. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient's medication list includes ramipril. To which drug class does it belong?","choices":["Angiotensin receptor blockers","ACE inhibitors","Dihydropyridine blockers","Beta blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The -pril suffix marks an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor; ramipril sits alongside lisinopril, enalapril, benazepril and quinapril. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What happens to renin release when renal perfusion falls?","choices":["It decreases","It stays constant","It stops completely","It increases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The renal baroreceptor responds to stretch of the afferent arteriole: less perfusion, more renin. Sympathetic activity at beta-1 receptors raises it too. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Compared with ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors, which statement about angiotensin receptor blockers is accurate?","choices":["They do not cause the dry cough","They cause more angioedema","They raise bradykinin further","They are safe late in pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They do not affect bradykinin metabolism, so cough does not occur and angioedema is less frequent, which is why patients may be switched to them. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A patient develops a persistent dry cough six weeks after starting lisinopril. What is the likely cause?","choices":["Bradykinin accumulating in the lungs","Angiotensin II acting on the airways","Aldosterone excess retaining fluid","Beta-2 blockade in the bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin and substance P accumulate because the converting enzyme no longer breaks them down; the cough appears between 1 week and 6 months. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
